  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Widespread rain tonight into Monday will help \nease ongoing drought conditions in some areas. Rain may be heavy\nat times tonight into Sunday, especially across Downeast Maine.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLopres off of Cape Cod will move northeast tonight, passing \njust south of the Gulf of Maine. Latest CAMS and some global \nmodels suggesting that rain will slow down and shouldn't push \nonto the coast until closer to 21z this afternoon. Given the \ntrack of the low think the best chance for pops will be north \nand east of the low center overnight with the bulk of the rain \noccurring to the east of MDI tonight. PW values in far sern area\nrising to close to 99th percentile with amounts between 1.50-2.00\ninches. Any rainfall will be helpful to ongoing drought conditions\ngiven that we will be in a cloudy cool pattern for the next \ncouple days. This will allow for rainfall to get into the soils,\nunlikely to help streamflows which are significantly below normal.\n\nWeak lopres will still be back over the western Gulf of Maine and \nbecoming absorbed by the occlusion around 12z. Although pwats remain \nhigh over srn and ern half of the area have reduced pops slightly in \nthe afternoon with parts of the area lacking forcing. Given the 160-\n180% of normal PWATs (95-99th percentile) showers may produce heavy \nrainfall at times. Showers will be more convective during the day so \nuncertain where the beneficial rainfall will occur. Then throughout \nSunday night, the low becomes stationary over Quebec as it rotates \nthe moisture through the state. By Monday, the low\nbegins to gradually move north which will decrease rain chances\nthroughout the day. However, best chance of showers and \nthunderstorms will be in the northern 1/2 of the CWA closer to \nthe vertically stacked low in Quebec.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Upper level low pressure keeps the chance of \nshowers going into midweek, especially across Northern Maine.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure stalls over Canada, slowly meandering to the east \nnext week. Looking at chances of rain showers in the north, on \nTuesday and Wednesday. There is some instability during the \nafternoons, with some models hinting at 500-1,000 J/kg of CAPE, \n7C/km low level lapse rates, and 30-40 kts of effective shear. \nPWAT values roughly 1.00-1.25\", so rain showers expected to be \nmostly light, with higher totals possible in any convection.\nThat being said, not anticipating severe weather. Temperatures \ndrop Tuesday into the upper-60s to low-70s, and raise back to \nthe 70s on Wednesday.",
